{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BwWoCAsXBLYJr85f4TDjQX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Now I can see the key differences between APC 0 and APC 300 kernel profiles.\n\n**APC 300 Top Kernels (Total Time):**\n1. `apc_apply_bus_kernel` - 604ms (20.7%) - 583 instances — THIS IS A TRACE GEN KERNEL, not STARK\n2. `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<bool=1>` - 374ms (12.8%) - 486 instances — LogUp GKR\n\n\n3. `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` - 231ms (7.9%) - 25 instances — Trace Commit\n4. `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<bool=1>` - 220ms (7.5%) - 539 instances — Round 0\n5. `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<bool=1>` - 195ms (6.7%) - 396 instances — Round 0\n6. `calculate_zero_hash` - 106ms (3.6%) - 584 instances\n\nThe remaining kernels are spread across trace commitment, constraint evaluation, and interaction computation phases, with the hash calculation being the smallest contributor in this top-6 list. Looking at the performance breakdown for Stacked Reduction kernels, I'm seeing a significant shift in behavior between APC 0 and APC 300. The `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel` is particularly concerning—it's scaling poorly with more instances, jumping from 3,281 calls taking 10ms up to 10,348 calls consuming 40ms, which suggests a 4x time increase despite only a 3x instance increase. Meanwhile, the block sum kernel is actually getting faster despite handling more work, which indicates different bottlenecks at play.\n\nThe real puzzle is the wall clock time: GPU kernels only add about 8ms of overhead going from APC 0 to APC 300, yet the total execution time balloons from 113ms to 317ms—a 204ms jump that's not accounted for by the GPU work alone. This suggests the bottleneck has shifted away from GPU computation toward something else, likely synchronization, memory transfers, or CPU-side scheduling overhead.\n\nNow looking at Round 0 performance, I'm seeing dramatic GPU time increases in the coset parallel kernels—the `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel` jumps from 30ms to 220ms, and the logup interaction kernel hits 195ms at APC 300. Meanwhile, the non-coset versions actually get faster, which is interesting.\n\nThe `calculate_zero_hash` kernel is a smaller piece of the puzzle—it's scaling linearly with AIR count and only accounts for about 3.6% of GPU time at APC 300.\n\nBut here's the critical finding: in `stacked_reduction`, the wall clock time nearly triples from 113ms to 317ms, yet the actual GPU kernel time barely budges from 93ms to 101ms. That's a massive gap between wall clock and kernel time, which points directly to CPU overhead and synchronization costs being the real bottleneck.\n\nLooking at the kernel launch counts, the problem becomes clear: at APC 0 there are roughly 6,700 kernel launches across the stacked reduction, but at APC 300 that explodes to nearly 18,000 launches. Each of those launches carries CPU-side overhead and synchronization penalties, which explains why the wall clock time degrades so much more than the actual GPU compute time.\n\nNow I need to figure out what's actually driving the performance regression. Looking at the constraint solving times, Round 0 is the worst offender—it's gone from 178ms to 664ms, nearly 3.7x slower. That's where I should focus. The LogUp GKR actually improved and WHIR improved too, so those optimizations are working, but Round 0 is completely overwhelming any gains elsewhere.\n\nThe stacked reduction is also concerning—wall clock time jumped from 113ms to 317ms even though the actual GPU kernel barely changed. That 204ms difference is pure host overhead from launching way more kernels (2.66x increase). But given that Round 0 is regressing by 486ms, that's the bigger problem to solve first. very small (single AIR), so many degenerate kernels are just trivial operations. The real bottleneck might be the same per-launch overhead issue as Round 0, though these degenerate cases could be easier to batch since they're essentially just copying or folding single elements.\n\nLooking at `calculate_zero_hash` next—it runs 584 times and takes 106ms at APC 300, which seems to be per-AIR work. But wait, Trace Commit actually improves from 518ms to 410ms as APC increases, so this kernel must be part of a phase that benefits from the larger problem size.\n\nNow I need to evaluate the main optimization strategies.\n\nStacked Reduction has a 204ms gap with minimal GPU time increase, suggesting the overhead is entirely on the host side and could benefit from batching smaller stacks together. Round 0 kernel batching is a bigger target at 486ms, but previous attempts at pipelining failed—the real approach might be grouping many small AIRs into single kernel launches. MLE Rounds is smaller at 70ms but follows the same pattern.\n\nI should dig into the stacked reduction code path first since it's the clearest untried opportunity with measurable overhead.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7096,"cache_read_input_tokens":55126,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7096},"output_tokens":31,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f2fc226-1649-44e7-a922-02ccd916e4bc","uuid":"b4ca2edb-a5aa-4e6a-a946-9fcdccbc61b4"}
