{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Mm5n3BLeA781RaohoTcyC","type":"tool_result","content":"50\tpub const STACKED_REDUCTION_S_DEG: usize = 2;\n51\t\n52\tpub struct StackedReductionGpu<D = Digest> {\n53\t    sm_count: u32,\n54\t\n55\t    l_skip: usize,\n56\t    n_stack: usize,\n57\t\n58\t    omega_skip: F,\n59\t    omega_skip_pows: Vec<F>,\n60\t    d_omega_skip_pows: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n61\t\n62\t    r_0: EF,\n63\t    d_lambda_pows: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n64\t    eq_const: EF,\n65\t\n66\t    pub(crate) stacked_per_commit: Vec<StackedPcsData2<D>>,\n67\t    d_q_widths: DeviceBuffer<u32>,\n68\t    q_width_max: u32,\n69\t    d_q_eval_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n70\t\n71\t    trace_ptrs: Vec<(\n72\t        *const F, /* trace_ptr */\n73\t        usize,    /* height */\n74\t        usize,    /* width */\n75\t    )>,\n76\t    unstacked_cols: Vec<UnstackedSlice>,\n77\t    d_unstacked_cols: DeviceBuffer<UnstackedSlice>,\n78\t    // boundary indices where heights change. all columns between two boundaries must have the same\n79\t    // height. We can have multiple chunks of the same height if that is needed to reduce peak GPU\n80\t    // memory.\n81\t    ht_diff_idxs: Vec<usize>,\n82\t    n_max: usize,\n83\t\n84\t    // Initially holds eq(r[1..=n], H_n) for n=0..=n_max but gets updated after each sumcheck round\n85\t    // by some custom folding\n86\t    eq_r_ns: EqEvalSegments<EF>,\n87\t\n88\t    // == After round 0 ==\n89\t    q_evals: Vec<DeviceBuffer<EF>>, // get width from stacked_per_commit\n90\t    // Stores folded eq values that won't change anymore (no more folding)\n91\t    // Corresponds to log_height in 0..l_skip+round-1 _before_ round `round`. Gets updated with one\n92\t    // new element after each round.\n93\t    eq_stable: Vec<EF>,\n94\t    k_rot_stable: Vec<EF>,\n95\t\n96\t    /// Stores the folded k_rot evaluations for `\\kappa_\\rot(x, r) = eq_n(rot^{-1}(x), r)` for each\n97\t    /// `n` after each round. We use the [EqEvalSegments] type to guard the segment-based\n98\t    /// memory layout.\n99\t    k_rot_ns: EqEvalSegments<EF>,\n100\t    /// Stores eq(u[1+n_T..round-1], b_{T,j}[..round-n_T-1])\n101\t    eq_ub_per_trace: Vec<EF>,\n102\t    d_eq_ub: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n103\t\n104\t    d_block_sums: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n105\t    d_accum: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n106\t    d_input_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n107\t    d_output_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<*mut EF>,\n108\t\n109\t    mem: MemTracker,\n110\t}\n111\t\n112\t/// A struct for holding stacked pcs data. We only need the `MerkleTreeGpu` from `StackedPcsDataGpu`\n113\t/// but we wrap the latter in an `Arc` to provide uniformity in dealing with common main and\n114\t/// preprocessed/cached traces. This struct stores the unstacked `traces`, in prismalinear\n115\t/// evaluation form, corresponding to the stacked pcs data.\n116\t///\n117\t/// Generic over the Merkle digest type `D`.  The default `D = Digest` preserves the existing\n118\t/// BabyBear-Poseidon2 behaviour.\n119\tpub struct StackedPcsData2<D = Digest> {\n120\t    pub(crate) inner: Arc<StackedPcsDataGpu<F, D>>,\n121\t    /// The unstacked traces corresponding to `inner`'s commitment.\n122\t    pub(crate) traces: Vec<DeviceMatrix<F>>,\n123\t}\n124\t\n125\timpl<D> StackedPcsData2<D> {\n126\t    /// # Safety\n127\t    /// `traces` must be the traces that were committed to in `pcs_data`.\n128\t    pub unsafe fn from_raw(\n129\t        pcs_data: Arc<StackedPcsDataGpu<F, D>>,\n130\t        traces: Vec<DeviceMatrix<F>>,\n131\t    ) -> Self {\n132\t        Self {\n133\t            inner: pcs_data,\n134\t            traces,\n135\t        }\n136\t    }\n137\t\n138\t    pub fn layout(&self) -> &StackedLayout {\n139\t        &self.inner.layout\n140\t    }\n141\t}\n142\t\n143\t/// Pointer with length to location in a big device buffer. The device buffer is identified by\n144\t/// `commit_idx`. Due to pecuarlities with `l_skip`, the length of the slice is defined as\n145\t/// `max(2^log_height, 2^l_skip)`. In other words, this is a pointer to the slice\n146\t/// `q[commit_idx].column(stacked_col_idx)[stacked_row_idx..stacked_row_idx + len]`.\n147\t///\n148\t/// # Safety\n149\t/// - this type is `repr(C)` as it will cross FFI boundaries for CUDA usage.\n150\t#[repr(C)]\n151\t#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\n152\tpub(crate) struct UnstackedSlice {\n153\t    commit_idx: u32,\n154\t    log_height: u32,\n155\t    stacked_row_idx: u32,\n156\t    stacked_col_idx: u32,\n157\t}\n158\t\n159\timpl<D> StackedReductionGpu<D> {\n160\t    fn log_stacked_height(&self, round: usize) -> usize {\n161\t        self.n_stack - (round - 1)\n162\t    }\n163\t\n164\t    fn stacked_height(&self, round: usize) -> usize {\n165\t        1 << self.log_stacked_height(round)\n166\t    }\n167\t\n168\t    /// Current maximum `n` supported by eq_r_ns\n169\t    fn cur_max_n(&self, round: usize) -> usize {\n170\t        self.n_max - (round - 1)\n171\t    }\n172\t}\n173\t\n174\t/// Batch sumcheck to reduce trace openings, including rotations, to stacked matrix opening.\n175\t///\n176\t/// The `stacked_matrix, stacked_layout` should be the result of stacking the `traces` with\n177\t/// parameters `l_skip` and `n_stack`.\n178\t#[allow(clippy::type_complexity)]\n179\t#[instrument(\n180\t    name = \"prover.openings.stacked_reduction\",\n181\t    level = \"info\",\n182\t    skip_all,\n183\t    fields(phase = \"prover\")\n184\t)]\n185\tpub fn prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu<HS, TS>(\n186\t    device: &GpuDevice,\n187\t    transcript: &mut TS,\n188\t    mpk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n189\t    ctx: ProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f2fc226-1649-44e7-a922-02ccd916e4bc","uuid":"16b25977-1493-4976-8231-a65569841c81","timestamp":"2026-04-12T20:31:49.058Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs","content":"pub const STACKED_REDUCTION_S_DEG: usize = 2;\n\npub struct StackedReductionGpu<D = Digest> {\n    sm_count: u32,\n\n    l_skip: usize,\n    n_stack: usize,\n\n    omega_skip: F,\n    omega_skip_pows: Vec<F>,\n    d_omega_skip_pows: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n\n    r_0: EF,\n    d_lambda_pows: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    eq_const: EF,\n\n    pub(crate) stacked_per_commit: Vec<StackedPcsData2<D>>,\n    d_q_widths: DeviceBuffer<u32>,\n    q_width_max: u32,\n    d_q_eval_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n\n    trace_ptrs: Vec<(\n        *const F, /* trace_ptr */\n        usize,    /* height */\n        usize,    /* width */\n    )>,\n    unstacked_cols: Vec<UnstackedSlice>,\n    d_unstacked_cols: DeviceBuffer<UnstackedSlice>,\n    // boundary indices where heights change. all columns between two boundaries must have the same\n    // height. We can have multiple chunks of the same height if that is needed to reduce peak GPU\n    // memory.\n    ht_diff_idxs: Vec<usize>,\n    n_max: usize,\n\n    // Initially holds eq(r[1..=n], H_n) for n=0..=n_max but gets updated after each sumcheck round\n    // by some custom folding\n    eq_r_ns: EqEvalSegments<EF>,\n\n    // == After round 0 ==\n    q_evals: Vec<DeviceBuffer<EF>>, // get width from stacked_per_commit\n    // Stores folded eq values that won't change anymore (no more folding)\n    // Corresponds to log_height in 0..l_skip+round-1 _before_ round `round`. Gets updated with one\n    // new element after each round.\n    eq_stable: Vec<EF>,\n    k_rot_stable: Vec<EF>,\n\n    /// Stores the folded k_rot evaluations for `\\kappa_\\rot(x, r) = eq_n(rot^{-1}(x), r)` for each\n    /// `n` after each round. We use the [EqEvalSegments] type to guard the segment-based\n    /// memory layout.\n    k_rot_ns: EqEvalSegments<EF>,\n    /// Stores eq(u[1+n_T..round-1], b_{T,j}[..round-n_T-1])\n    eq_ub_per_trace: Vec<EF>,\n    d_eq_ub: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n\n    d_block_sums: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    d_accum: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n    d_input_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n    d_output_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<*mut EF>,\n\n    mem: MemTracker,\n}\n\n/// A struct for holding stacked pcs data. We only need the `MerkleTreeGpu` from `StackedPcsDataGpu`\n/// but we wrap the latter in an `Arc` to provide uniformity in dealing with common main and\n/// preprocessed/cached traces. This struct stores the unstacked `traces`, in prismalinear\n/// evaluation form, corresponding to the stacked pcs data.\n///\n/// Generic over the Merkle digest type `D`.  The default `D = Digest` preserves the existing\n/// BabyBear-Poseidon2 behaviour.\npub struct StackedPcsData2<D = Digest> {\n    pub(crate) inner: Arc<StackedPcsDataGpu<F, D>>,\n    /// The unstacked traces corresponding to `inner`'s commitment.\n    pub(crate) traces: Vec<DeviceMatrix<F>>,\n}\n\nimpl<D> StackedPcsData2<D> {\n    /// # Safety\n    /// `traces` must be the traces that were committed to in `pcs_data`.\n    pub unsafe fn from_raw(\n        pcs_data: Arc<StackedPcsDataGpu<F, D>>,\n        traces: Vec<DeviceMatrix<F>>,\n    ) -> Self {\n        Self {\n            inner: pcs_data,\n            traces,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn layout(&self) -> &StackedLayout {\n        &self.inner.layout\n    }\n}\n\n/// Pointer with length to location in a big device buffer. The device buffer is identified by\n/// `commit_idx`. Due to pecuarlities with `l_skip`, the length of the slice is defined as\n/// `max(2^log_height, 2^l_skip)`. In other words, this is a pointer to the slice\n/// `q[commit_idx].column(stacked_col_idx)[stacked_row_idx..stacked_row_idx + len]`.\n///\n/// # Safety\n/// - this type is `repr(C)` as it will cross FFI boundaries for CUDA usage.\n#[repr(C)]\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\npub(crate) struct UnstackedSlice {\n    commit_idx: u32,\n    log_height: u32,\n    stacked_row_idx: u32,\n    stacked_col_idx: u32,\n}\n\nimpl<D> StackedReductionGpu<D> {\n    fn log_stacked_height(&self, round: usize) -> usize {\n        self.n_stack - (round - 1)\n    }\n\n    fn stacked_height(&self, round: usize) -> usize {\n        1 << self.log_stacked_height(round)\n    }\n\n    /// Current maximum `n` supported by eq_r_ns\n    fn cur_max_n(&self, round: usize) -> usize {\n        self.n_max - (round - 1)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Batch sumcheck to reduce trace openings, including rotations, to stacked matrix opening.\n///\n/// The `stacked_matrix, stacked_layout` should be the result of stacking the `traces` with\n/// parameters `l_skip` and `n_stack`.\n#[allow(clippy::type_complexity)]\n#[instrument(\n    name = \"prover.openings.stacked_reduction\",\n    level = \"info\",\n    skip_all,\n    fields(phase = \"prover\")\n)]\npub fn prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu<HS, TS>(\n    device: &GpuDevice,\n    transcript: &mut TS,\n    mpk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    ctx: ProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,","numLines":140,"startLine":50,"totalLines":1043}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FrEQnzgUDy7tZkoWvjq6kQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me also understand the `fold_mle_evals` function:\n\nAt line 986-994, after the GPU-side fold operations, there's a CPU-side loop over all `unstacked_cols`:\n```rust\nfor (s, eq_ub) in zip(&self.unstacked_cols, &mut self.eq_ub_per_trace) {\n\n\n```\n\nInside this loop, the code checks if we've moved past a column's log height and updates the evaluation basis accordingly by multiplying in the appropriate bit value. With 623 AIRs and potentially many unstacked columns per AIR, this CPU-side update could add up. Let me figure out how many unstacked columns we're actually dealing with—each trace column that isn't stacked contributes one entry here.\n\nAt APC 300, we're looking at over 106,000 columns total, so this loop runs more than 106K times. Fortunately, each iteration is just a single multiplication, so the computational cost should be manageable.\n\nNow I'm consolidating what I've found and putting together an optimization strategy. The real bottleneck appears to be in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval`, where for each height group we're doing a GPU memset, optionally copying data from host to device, launching a kernel that uses atomic operations to accumulate results, and then synchronously copying the accumulated values back to the host. This synchronous device-to-host transfer is happening repeatedly and is likely where we're losing performance.\n\nSince we have N height groups and S rounds, we're hitting N × S × 2 synchronization points just from the memset and host transfer operations. The key insight is that both kernels are already using atomic operations to accumulate into the same buffer, so all windows within a round can actually share a single accumulator—the final result is just the sum across all windows anyway.\n\nThe optimization strategy is to consolidate this: upload the entire `eq_ub_per_trace` data once, do a single memset per round, launch all the kernels back-to-back with pointer arithmetic to handle different parts of the data, and then do a single device-to-host transfer per round. This would eliminate (N-1) × S unnecessary memsets and (N-1) × S redundant copies back to the host.\n\nWith around 100+ height groups at APC 300, this could significantly reduce synchronization overhead. I need to check the round 0 code as well since it follows the same per-column loop pattern, which could also be contributing to the sync overhead.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5094,"cache_read_input_tokens":78975,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5094},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f2fc226-1649-44e7-a922-02ccd916e4bc","uuid":"8b996278-16e3-4e7c-a9e9-2e16810a73ed"}
