{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DYkvKFtvze9EaeDy8WSxHV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013G5mUv39PGJ4D4qFWig6Sm","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-12-2330-batch-stacked-reduction-mle-sync\n\nThe stacked reduction MLE rounds in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` perform a synchronous D2H copy (`to_host()` with full GPU pipeline drain) per height group per round. With ~400+ height groups and 17 MLE rounds, this creates ~7,000+ pipeline-draining sync points per segment. Since both the regular and degenerate CUDA kernels already use atomic accumulation into a shared `d_accum` buffer, and the final result is a sum across all windows, these per-window syncs are unnecessary — a single `fill_zero` + `to_host` per round suffices. This eliminates the dominant source of CPU-GPU synchronization overhead, which profiling shows accounts for the majority of the 287ms MLE rounds time at APC 300 (vs ~60ms of actual GPU kernel time).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1241,"cache_read_input_tokens":110544,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1241},"output_tokens":47,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f2fc226-1649-44e7-a922-02ccd916e4bc","uuid":"bcfddb06-95c2-4f16-b17f-8086f3a92e0e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013G5mUv39PGJ4D4qFWig6Sm","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f2fc226-1649-44e7-a922-02ccd916e4bc","uuid":"095e5ae0-c087-4c79-996c-4f75c9e8085b","timestamp":"2026-04-12T20:36:50.095Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-12-2330-batch-stacked-reduction-mle-sync\n\nThe stacked reduction MLE rounds in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` perform a synchronous D2H copy (`to_host()` with full GPU pipeline drain) per height group per round. With ~400+ height groups and 17 MLE rounds, this creates ~7,000+ pipeline-draining sync points per segment. Since both the regular and degenerate CUDA kernels already use atomic accumulation into a shared `d_accum` buffer, and the final result is a sum across all windows, these per-window syncs are unnecessary — a single `fill_zero` + `to_host` per round suffices. This eliminates the dominant source of CPU-GPU synchronization overhead, which profiling shows accounts for the majority of the 287ms MLE rounds time at APC 300 (vs ~60ms of actual GPU kernel time).\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-12-2245-parallelize-round0-cpu-postprocess","+# 2026-04-12-2330-batch-stacked-reduction-mle-sync"," ","-Round 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 spends ~200ms of its 662ms total on serial CPU post-processing (transpose + iDFT + polynomial construction), executed once per AIR × 3 calls inside a sequential loop over 623 AIRs. This work is embarrassingly parallel — each AIR's post-processing is independent. Restructuring the loop to collect raw GPU results first, then batch-process them with rayon's `par_iter` across 32 cores, should reduce this CPU overhead from ~200ms to ~7-13ms, yielding an estimated 28% improvement on Round 0 and 7-8% on STARK excl trace.","+The stacked reduction MLE rounds in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` perform a synchronous D2H copy (`to_host()` with full GPU pipeline drain) per height group per round. With ~400+ height groups and 17 MLE rounds, this creates ~7,000+ pipeline-draining sync points per segment. Since both the regular and degenerate CUDA kernels already use atomic accumulation into a shared `d_accum` buffer, and the final result is a sum across all windows, these per-window syncs are unnecessary — a single `fill_zero` + `to_host` per round suffices. This eliminates the dominant source of CPU-GPU synchronization overhead, which profiling shows accounts for the majority of the 287ms MLE rounds time at APC 300 (vs ~60ms of actual GPU kernel time)."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-12-2245-parallelize-round0-cpu-postprocess\n\nRound 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 spends ~200ms of its 662ms total on serial CPU post-processing (transpose + iDFT + polynomial construction), executed once per AIR × 3 calls inside a sequential loop over 623 AIRs. This work is embarrassingly parallel — each AIR's post-processing is independent. Restructuring the loop to collect raw GPU results first, then batch-process them with rayon's `par_iter` across 32 cores, should reduce this CPU overhead from ~200ms to ~7-13ms, yielding an estimated 28% improvement on Round 0 and 7-8% on STARK excl trace.\n"}}
